[08:05.45]Passage One
[08:07.25]Teenagers have fewer friends than 20 years ago, research has shown¡ªdespite the increasing popularity of social media. 
[08:16.27]A study carried out by experts at the University of Queensland in Australia found teens felt less lonely than two decades ago¡ªbut that they have poorer social networks. 
[08:28.68]A survey of more than 285,000 US high school students carried out between 1991 and 2012 found young people had fewer friends with whom to interact, but less desire for more friends. 
[08:44.97]The Globe and Mail quotes the findings of the research printed in the Personality and Social Psychology Bulletin, which suggest economic changes may have prompted increased individuality among young people.
[08:59.29]The authors behind the study wrote: ¡°Greater economic opportunities offer individuals more latitude to manage their own money, decide whom to date and decide whom to marry, reducing the influence of kin and giving people more autonomy, which may increase individualism. 
[09:20.32]Economic changes lead to increased individuality, which could lead to decreasing interest in friends, increasing self-reliance, increasing self-esteem and decreasing loneliness.¡±
[09:34.01]Lead researcher, Dr. David Clark, said further research on cultures outside of the US would help determine if modernization is the root cause of the observed results. 
[09:45.42]He said: ¡°If other cultures show the same pattern of reduced loneliness in the face of poorer social networks, this would support the idea that modernization is responsible.¡± 
[09:58.01]The findings come after a series of studies, which have shown that the growing use of social networking sites has had a negative effect on the quality of people's friendships.
[10:11.60]Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[10:16.73]9. What finding has been made through the survey of US high school students?
[10:37.78]10. What is likely to be the cause of increased individuality among young people?
[10:58.30]11. What further study does the lead researcher say is needed for drawing the conclusion?
[11:19.22]Passage Two
[11:21.22]A new program called AdelanTECH Leadership is helping students in the rural community¡ªwhich is just an hour from Silicon Valley¡ªget web development training and leadership skills, so they too, can work in tech. 
[11:36.29]Salinas' population is 75% Hispanic, a vastly underrepresented minority group in Silicon Valley. 
[11:45.04]¡°We see this as part and parcel of our broader diversity outreach,¡± said Ken McNeely, president of AT&T California, which is footing the bill for the 10-month program. 
[11:57.97]¡°We know that STEM jobs are solving the economy...Where's the next new talent? How can we train the workforce of the future?¡± This is one attempt.
[12:09.05]United Farm Workers, a national union for those in agriculture, helped identify twelve participants. 
[12:16.81]It looked for students enrolled in a two- or four-year institution, who had a demonstrated background in math or science. 
[12:25.36]Starting in July, students commit 10 hours a week to online web development courses through Udacity, which will provide them with certification. 
[12:35.80]They also devote one Saturday a month to CORO leadership training, a chance for them to engage with one another. 
[12:43.72]¡°We hope that not only are they prepared to work at a company like AT&T but also at companies like Uber, Facebook, which are also significant employers in the California economy,¡± said McNeely. 
[12:57.30]18-year-old Luis Villalobos said he found out about the program from his father who is a farmer involved with United Farm Workers. 
[13:06.37]¡°My parents don't really know much about technology,¡± Villalobos told CNN Money. 
[13:12.53]¡°They're immigrant farm workers that didn't get much education.¡±
