[19:01.51]Recording Two
[19:03.57]Many people who drink alcohol also like to smoke cigarettes. 
[19:08.21]Drinking and smoking seem to be closely linked. 
[19:11.52]Some people might even say they go together hand-in-hand. 
[19:16.17]But this may be more than just a mixing of two fairly common activities. 
[19:21.86]New research has looked more closely at the relationship between cigarettes and alcohol. 
[19:27.79]And the relationship is one of equal dependence. 
[19:32.37]Smoking and drinking seem to feed on each other. 
[19:36.39]These are the exact words of Mahesh Thakkar. 
[19:40.26]He is head of research in the Department of Neurology at the University of Missouri's School of Medicine. 
[19:48.18]Thakkar explains that when a person drinks alcohol he gets sleepy. 
[19:53.56]He says a drug in cigarettes fights that sleepy feeling. 
[19:58.08]That drug, nicotine, can be addictive; smokers need more cigarettes because of their body's growing dependence on the drug. 
[20:07.48]So, if a person smokes, then he or she is much more likely to drink alcohol, and vice versa. 
[20:15.46]Researchers already knew that people who use alcohol often smoke. 
[20:20.46]In fact, earlier research shows that more than 85% of alcohol-dependent American adults also depend on nicotine. 
[20:30.09]Thakkar's earlier research showed that nicotine combined with alcohol stimulates what he calls the ¡°reward center¡± of the brain. 
[20:39.06]However, the new study shows a dependent relationship between the substances. 
[20:45.19]Thakkar says his team finds that nicotine weakens the sleep-causing effects of alcohol. 
[20:52.11]It does this by activating an area of the brain called the basal forebrain. 
[20:57.53]According to the psychology definition website, that area is responsible for memory, learning and attention. 
[21:05.90]For this new experiment, Thakkar and his team used equipment that measures brain activity in rats. 
[21:13.01]They injected the rats with both nicotine and alcohol, and then studied brain activity in the animals as they slept. 
[21:22.06]The researchers found that nicotine goes through the basal forebrain and cancels out the sleep-causing effects of alcohol. 
[21:30.32]The World Health Organization says 7 million people die every year from alcohol and nicotine use. 
[21:37.81]Mahesh Thakker and his team identified why alcohol use and smoking are often linked. 
[21:44.68]He says this knowledge may help people break their addictions to alcohol and nicotine.
[21:52.40]Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.
[21:58.36]19. What's the relationship between cigarettes and alcohol?
[22:18.70]20. What does Thakkar's team find out about nicotine?
